.TH "ifconfig" 1G "5 October 2026" GINI "gRouter Commands"

.SH NAME
ifconfig \- setup network interfaces at gRouter

.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
ifconfig show [brief | verbose]
ifconfig down | up tunN
ifconfig mod tunN [-mtu value] [-netmask mask]
ifconfig del tunN
ifconfig add tunN -dstip peer_ip -dstport peer_port -addr IP_address
             -hwaddr MAC_addr [-srcport local_port] [-netmask mask]
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION

Each link from the router to another element is one interface: tun1, tun2, and so on. When you
press Run, gBuilder creates them for you, gives each its IP and MAC address, and connects it to
the element at the other end of the link. So you will mostly use ifconfig to LOOK at interfaces
and to take one down and up; you rarely need add.

.B show
prints the interface table: device, state (U up, D down), IP address, MAC address and MTU.
.B show verbose
adds the interface number and the socket and thread behind each one.
.B show brief
is the same as plain show.

.B down
disables an interface without removing it: nothing is sent or received on it, so the link
behaves as if it were unplugged, and its routes stay in the table pointing at a dead link. That
is a good way to watch what fails, and what does not, when a link breaks.
.B up
brings it back.

.B mod
changes an interface's MTU, the largest IP packet it will send without fragmenting it, or its
subnet mask.

.B del
removes an interface for good, together with the routes that use it. It does not come back
until the next Run.

.B add
creates an interface by hand. A tun interface carries Ethernet frames inside UDP datagrams to
the element at the other end: -dstip and -dstport say where to send them, -srcport is the local
UDP port to receive on, -addr and -hwaddr are the interface's own IP and MAC addresses, and
-netmask is its subnet mask (255.255.255.0 if omitted). The router uses the mask to work out its
subnet's broadcast address, which a routing protocol's messages are sent to. The
commands gBuilder ran for this router are in its configuration file, which is the best model to
copy: type cat /run/r1.conf (with your router's name), since this console hands commands it
does not know to the Linux shell. add does not create a route; use route add for that, and use
mod to set the MTU.

The router also knows three older interface types from GINI's earlier versions: ethN (a
Unix-socket link), tap0 (a link to the host) and raw. GINI's current topologies use only tun.

.SH EXAMPLES

Look at the interfaces:
.RS
.nf
ifconfig show
.fi
.RE

Unplug the link on tun2, watch the traffic stop, then plug it back in:
.RS
.nf
ifconfig down tun2
ifconfig show
ifconfig up tun2
.fi
.RE

Lower the MTU on tun1, so larger packets have to be fragmented:
.RS
.nf
ifconfig mod tun1 -mtu 576
.fi
.RE

